@page "/large-data"

<PageTitle>Large data · RioCharts for Blazor and .NET</PageTitle>

<h1>Large data</h1>
<p class="lead">
    Charts with hundreds of thousands of points stay fast in Blazor: pass columns directly and let LTTB downsampling keep the shape at the
    plot's resolution. Draw the series on a canvas when the DOM gets heavy; zooming in downsamples only the visible points, so detail appears.
</p>

<Example Title="200 000 points" Code="@BigCode">
    <Description><p>A month of server response times, one every 13 seconds, running in WebAssembly in your browser. Drag to zoom in.</p></Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="double" Height="280" Zoomable="true" SeriesLayer="_layer" YAxis="Ms">
            <LineSeries Name="Response time" Data="_big" StrokeWidth="1.25" Format="0 ms" />
        </RioChart>
        <p class="note">
            Series layer: @_layer
            <button class="small" @onclick="() => _layer = _layer == SeriesLayer.Svg ? SeriesLayer.Canvas : SeriesLayer.Svg">Switch to @(_layer == SeriesLayer.Svg ? "canvas" : "SVG")</button>
        </p>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<Example Title="100 000 points in a scatter" Code="@ScatterCode">
    <Description>
        <p>
            Minutes on the site against order value for 100 000 orders. Points are data, so a scatter is never downsampled, but where markers
            pile up only three are drawn per square one marker radius wide: by then the translucent markers are solid, so the picture is the same.
            Outliers and sparse areas keep every point; the trend line uses them all. Drag to zoom into the dense corner.
        </p>
    </Description>
    <ChildContent>
        <RioChart TItem="double" Height="320" Zoomable="true" SeriesLayer="SeriesLayer.Canvas" XAxis="Minutes" YAxis="Euros">
            <ScatterSeries Name="Orders" Data="_orders" MarkerSize="4" TrendLine="true" Format="0 €" />
        </RioChart>
    </ChildContent>
</Example>

<h2>Guidelines</h2>
<ul>
    <li>Use <code>SeriesData.From(times, values)</code> for big series: no per-item selectors, no boxing.</li>
    <li>Downsampling is automatic (twice the plot width, four times on a canvas); set <code>DownsampleThreshold</code> to change it.</li>
    <li>Scatter series are thinned instead: above the same threshold, at most three markers in each small square. Hover and tooltips use the drawn points.</li>
    <li><code>SeriesLayer.Canvas</code> keeps axes, labels and annotations in SVG and draws only the series on a canvas.</li>
    <li>Update with a new <code>SeriesData</code> or a <code>DataVersion</code>; for streams use a <code>SeriesBuffer</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="canvas">Canvas compared with SVG</h2>
<p>
    With <code>SeriesLayer.Canvas</code> the series are painted on a canvas laid over the SVG chart; axes, grid, legend, labels and
    annotations stay in SVG. Tooltips, clicks, zoom and pan work the same, since they use the chart's own hit testing. These differ:
</p>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="docs">
    <tr><th>What</th><th>On a canvas</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Data labels and annotations</td><td>Drawn under the series, also annotations with <code>Layer="AnnotationLayer.Above"</code>. A line can cross a label.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Highlight</td><td>Hovering a legend item or <code>HighlightSeriesAsync</code> does not fade the other series.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Entry fade</td><td>The series appear at once instead of fading in.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Downsampling</td><td>Line and area series keep four times the plot width (at least 2000 points) instead of twice.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Styling</td><td>Colors and theme tokens apply, and the canvas repaints when the theme changes; your own CSS rules on the series' SVG elements do not.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Image export</td><td><code>ExportSvgAsync</code> writes the series as a PNG image inside the SVG; <code>ExportPngAsync</code> is unaffected. Server export with RioCharts.Skia always draws vectors.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Static rendering</td><td>Static server rendering and prerendering draw the series in SVG; the canvas takes over once the chart runs interactively.</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Maps</td><td>Drawn in SVG, countries and bubbles; <code>SeriesLayer</code> has no effect.</td></tr>
</table>
</div>
<p>Use SVG unless the series have many thousands of drawn shapes; most charts never need the canvas.</p>

@code {
    private static readonly AxisOptions Ms = new() { Title = "ms" };
    private static readonly SeriesData _big = Create();
    private static readonly AxisOptions Minutes = new() { Title = "Minutes on the site" };
    private static readonly AxisOptions Euros = new() { Title = "Order value, €" };
    private static readonly SeriesData _orders = CreateOrders();

    private static SeriesData CreateOrders()
    {
        var (minutes, values) = Orders(100_000);
        return SeriesData.From(minutes, values);
    }
    private SeriesLayer _layer = SeriesLayer.Canvas;

    private static SeriesData Create()
    {
        var (times, values) = Big(200_000);
        return SeriesData.From(times, values);
    }

    private const string BigCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="double" Zoomable="true" SeriesLayer="SeriesLayer.Canvas" YAxis="@(new AxisOptions { Title = "ms" })">
            <LineSeries Name="Response time" Data="SeriesData.From(times, values)" StrokeWidth="1.25" Format="0 ms" />
        </RioChart>
        """;

    private const string ScatterCode = """
        <RioChart TItem="double" Zoomable="true" SeriesLayer="SeriesLayer.Canvas"
                  XAxis="@(new AxisOptions { Title = "Minutes on the site" })" YAxis="@(new AxisOptions { Title = "Order value, €" })">
            <ScatterSeries Name="Orders" Data="SeriesData.From(minutes, values)" MarkerSize="4" TrendLine="true" Format="0 €" />
        </RioChart>
        """;
}
